sentences.l:
If any either of cattle a herd or a great flock of sheep
we find, let not anyone, through evil wanton-follies,
either an ox or any sheep kill;

But when of drink and of food they had put away desire,
remembering then indeed, their dear companions they wept for,
whom Scylla ate, from the hollow ship having seized them;

But when indeed through the island going I had escaped my companions,
having washed my hands, where there was a shelter from the wind,
I prayed to all the gods who hold Olympus;

At once, of the Sun’s cattle having driven off the best,
from near by, for not far from the dark-prowed ship
were grazing crumple-horned, fine cattle, broad-browed;
